$NetBSD$

To run the server:

1. Write ${PKG_SYSCONFDIR}/stns.conf.  An example is installed as
   ${PREFIX}/share/examples/stnsd/stns.conf, and the file holds password
   hashes, so it is created root owned and mode 0600.  Keep it that way.

2. Check it, then enable the daemon:

	${PREFIX}/sbin/stnsd -t
	echo stnsd=YES >> /etc/rc.conf
	${RCD_SCRIPTS_DIR}/stnsd start

   The rc.d script checks the configuration before every start and every
   reload, and "${RCD_SCRIPTS_DIR}/stnsd reload" rereads it without
   dropping the socket.

The daemon listens on port 1104 and speaks plain HTTP.  It has no TLS of
its own: put relayd(8) or nginx in front of it if it is to be reached
across anything but a trusted network.

Hosts resolving accounts from it need a client: security/nss_stns on
NetBSD, STNS/libnss on Linux.
